class Solution {
  public int digitWorkshopBossReceipt(int[] readings) {
    int[] a=readings; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {for(int x:a){x=Math.abs(x);int s=0;while(x>0){s+=x%10;x/=10;}out.add(s);}}
    int answer=0;for(int i=0;i<out.size();i++)answer+=(i+1)*out.get(i);return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build digitSums

Find the digit sum of each number in `readings`. Ignore a minus sign, then add the digits. For example, -305 gives 3 + 0 + 5 = 8. The digit sum of 0 is 0. Keep the answers in input order.

Return the answer

Multiply each number in `digitSums` by its position, counting from 1: the first number by 1, the second by 2, and so on. Add those products. Store the sum in `weightedTotal`. Complete `digitWorkshopBossReceipt` so it returns `weightedTotal`. Return it, do not print it.

Why this works

Each value in `digitSums` follows task 1. The later tasks use only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n); extra memory, including the helper list, O(n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

